{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Security deposit transfer to a new owner on sale","requirement":"When a rental property is sold or transferred, the new owner must obtain the tenant's security deposit and accrued interest and is responsible for investing it, giving all notices, paying interest and returning the deposit plus accrued earnings.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Residential rental property with a security deposit collected by the former owner; the new owner is responsible whether or not the deposit and interest are transferred.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Residential rental units with a security deposit"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Civil penalty of not less than $500 and not more than $2,000 per offense for willfully and intentionally withholding a deposit from a tenant receiving state or federal financial assistance; unlawful use of deposit monies is a disorderly persons offense with a fine of at least $200, imprisonment up to 30 days, or both.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-21","quoted_span":"If the property is sold or transferred it is the duty of the new owner to obtain the security deposit, plus accrued interest on the tenant’s deposit, that was collected by the former owner.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act good cause requirement","requirement":"A landlord may recover possession of a residential dwelling only with the tenant's consent or through the legal eviction process, and must have good cause to evict or fail to renew a lease. Each cause except nonpayment of rent must be detailed in a written notice.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Dwelling units used as a residence, under written or oral leases; each cause for eviction except nonpayment of rent must be described in detail in a written notice to the tenant.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Residential dwelling units; written or oral leases"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"No residential landlord may evict or fail to renew a lease, whether it is a written or an oral lease without good cause.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act rent increase notice and unconscionability","requirement":"A landlord cannot raise rent mid-lease; the old lease must be terminated and the tenant offered a new lease at the increased rent with written notice. An increase must not be unconscionable, and a notice to quit and rent-increase notice are required before eviction.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Residential tenancies under a written or oral lease; increases must comply with any municipal ordinances governing rent increases.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Residential leases; municipal rent control ordinances may also govern increases"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"A landlord cannot raise the rent mid-lease term. The old lease must be terminated, and the new lease must have the increased rental payment.","interaction":"Rent increases must comply with any municipal ordinances governing rent increases.","confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Newly Constructed Multiple Dwellings Law 30-year rent control exemption","requirement":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings are exempt from local rent control ordinances for 30 years after construction is completed. Rents subsidized by governmental funding may also be exempt from local rent control.","key_value":"30 years following completion of construction","coverage_conditions":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings in municipalities that have rent control or rent leveling ordinances.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":"construction_date","min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings"},"exemptions":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings for 30 years after completion of construction; rents subsidized by governmental funding may also be exempt from local rent control ordinances.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:42-84.5","quoted_span":"newly constructed multiple dwellings shall be exempt from any local rent control ordinances for a period of 30 years following completion of construction of the building","interaction":"The State has no laws that establish, govern or control rents; municipalities may pass rent control ordinances, from which this law exempts new buildings for 30 years.","confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Ban on rent increases for conversion costs","requirement":"Rents may not be increased to cover costs resulting solely from converting a building to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership. Increases covering the cost of new services or amenities are still allowed.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All tenants in a building converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership of units, regardless of whether they are eligible for protected tenancy as senior citizens or disabled persons.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Buildings converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership"},"exemptions":"Increases to cover the cost of new services or amenities are not prohibited.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.31","quoted_span":"If a building is converted to a condominium or cooperative form of ownership, or to fee simple ownership of units, rents may not be increased to cover cos ts resulting solely from the conversion","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"application_screening_fees","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Landlord may charge tenants for credit reports and application fees","requirement":"A landlord may charge a prospective tenant for the cost of a credit or tenant screening report and may also request reasonable rental application fees or employment verification.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Prospective tenants screened by landlords through credit bureaus or tenant screening agencies; the text states no cap on these charges.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":null,"quoted_span":"The landlord is allowed to charge the tenant for the cost of the report. The landlord may also request reasonable rental application fees or employment verification.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"screening_restrictions","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Law Against Discrimination protection of source of lawful income in rental housing","requirement":"A landlord or rental agency may not refuse to rent or discriminate in renting housing because of a person's source of lawful income or other listed protected characteristics, and must give equal treatment in the rental of housing.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All landlord-tenant relationships in New Jersey, including source of lawful income as a protected characteristic.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"Source of lawful income is a protected characteristic"},"exemptions":"Two-family owner-occupied dwellings; rooms in an owner- or resident-occupied single home; residences planned exclusively for and occupied by one sex, as it pertains to familial status.","penalty":"Landlords who violate the law are subject to fines of up to $10,000 for a first offense, and may be required to pay monetary damages and to rent the unit to the complainant.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 10:5-12","quoted_span":"The New Jersey Law Against Discrimination (LAD), N.J.S.A. 10:5-12(g) to -(h), prohibits discrimination when selling or renting property and requires equal treatment in the sale or rental of housing regardless of race, creed, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, marital status, civil union status, domestic partnership status, familial status, affectional or sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, mental and physical disability, nationality, or source of lawful income.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
